based on the real-life operation dynamo  Christopher Nolan's Dunkirk follows the  evacuation of over 300 thousand soldiers  from a French beach in the opening  stages of World War two visually it's a  masterpiece and one of the most unique  war films to date and for the most part  the movie sticks religiously to the  facts taking inspiration from actual war  photos to frame the action even that  weird-looking sailboat at the end was  there but it doesn't get everything  right you're all the ways Dunkirk lied  about those historical events Nazi nose  cones Christopher Nolan was actually  pretty candid about this historical  discrepancy in the movie the German  planes at Tom Hardy's character fights  have their nose cones painted yellow  that's something the Germans actually  did in the war just not until about a  month after Dunkirk according to Nolan  the reason he decided to adopt the color  scheme for the planes in the movie was a  simple matter of identity during the  dogfights the yellow identifies the  planes as German at a glance  making it easier for audiences to keep  up with the action without trying to  figure out who was who the whole time as  a work definitely French destroyer  Nolan also came clean about another  historical alteration he had to make for  the sake of the film namely filming on a  French destroyer instead of a British  destroyer Nolan said for someone  extremely knowledgeable they'll see the  differences it's a bigger boat and  longer but we dressed it to make it look  like a British destroyer besides being a  detail that only a hardcore history buff  would ever notice the important takeaway  here is that Christopher Nolan filmed on  a real destroyer instead of falling back  on CGI will happily take an extra  smokestack over another battleship Thank  You farriers fuel tank when we first  meet Tom Hardy's character Ferrier he's  already in the air and the movie  announces in a story lasts an hour it's  an intense story arc and we're right  there in the cockpit with him feeling  the pressure as that fuel starts to run  out  he starts with 50 gallons and has to pop  his reserve tank by the time he reaches  Dunkirk  but that's a problem a main fighter used  by the Royal Air Force at Dunkirk was  the Supermarine Spitfire mark 1 there  were fast agile planes with a fuel tank  that could hold 85 gallons giving them a  395 Mile combat range in contrast the  evacuation route the ship's used across  the English Channel was about 55 miles  even accounting for the dogfights and  switchbacks to chase the bomber going  after the British destroyer he should  have had plenty of fuel Spitfires could  fly 220 miles per hour on only 20  gallons of fuel per hour over more time  running out of fuels totally plausible  just probably not in one hour civilian  volunteers we saw the movie how mark  Rylands his character mr. Dawson risked  life and limb to pilot his small sailing  out across the channel and into Dunkirk  in real life a fleet of about 700  private craft assisted the evacuation  effort they were called the little ships  of Dunkirk Jim yeah they were pretty  awesome and it's true that some boat  owners took up the call and sail their  own ships for the evacuation just not  very many according to the association  of Dunkirk little ships the vast  majority of those boats were  requisitioned by the British government  at which point naval officers were  assigned to sail them to Dunkirk and  speaking of the little ships the cavalry  arrives it makes a great heartwarming  climax when the flotilla of little ships  sails up to the beach at Dunkirk but  there was no heroic moment where the  ships arrived to save the day  Operation Dynamo lasted from May 26th of  June for both the little ships and the  British Navy work tirelessly over all 12  of those days and while the movie makes  out the arrival of little ships at the  turning point they didn't rescue the  majority of the soldiers out of the 338  thousand men rescued 239,000 were taken  off the MOL by Navy ships less than  100,000 were picked up off the beaches  and many of those were ferried out to  larger ships by the ship's own Mike  craft only six thousand soldiers made  the trip across the channel in one of  the little ships their contribution to  the evacuation effort can't be  understated of course but it can be  overstated and it often has been the  French Connection at one point in the  movie we see French soldiers clamoring  to get out to the east mole that leads  to the evacuation ships only to get  turned away by a British officer who  tells them that the British ships are  for the British soldiers that's about  the last we hear from the French until  the end when commander Bolton says that  he'll stay behind to help evacuate the  French but the French army was front and  center in Dunkirk of the 338 thousand  soldiers taken off the beach 123  thousand were French soldiers that's  more than a third of everyone who got  rescued as for the French soldiers who  didn't make it off they weren't fighting  to get on an evac vote no they were back  in the actual town of Dunkirk holding  back the Germans while the evacuation  took place estimates put French  casualties at Dunkirk between 50,000 and  90,000 and thousands more were taken  captive because they took a stand and  dug in defend the other soldiers on the  beach  ruins of Dunkirk the majority of  Christopher Nolan's Dunkirk takes place  on the beach at sea and in the air the  trifecta of nolan time that makes up the  narrative of the film however we do get  a few glimpses of the town itself mostly  in the beginning what we see is an  apocalyptic vacancy but no real  destruction except for the pile of  sandbags in one scene it looks less like  a warzone and more like everybody just  left for lunch the exact same time what  should we be seeing utter devastation  between the Luftwaffe bombers and  shelling from the German approach from  the east  Dunkirk was brought to its knees during  the evacuation Christopher Nolan  actually filmed in the town of Dunkirk  which lends historical presence to the  film but it doesn't relate the true  gravity of the situation thousands of  everyday lives ground to a halt the  minute allied troops retreated to  Dunkirk and it took years after the war  to rebuild those lives to something  resembling normalcy thanks for watching  click the looper icon to subscribe to  our YouTube channel let's check out all  this cool stuff we know you'll love to  [Music]

